In the semiempirical (liquid-drop) model, the nuclear binding energy is

\[
B(A,Z)=a_vA-a_sA^{2/3}
-a_c\frac{Z(Z-1)}{A^{1/3}}
-a_a\frac{(A-2Z)^2}{A}
+\delta(A,Z),
\]

where the terms are, respectively, the volume, surface, Coulomb, asymmetry, and pairing contributions. Typically,

\[
\delta(A,Z)=
\begin{cases}
+a_pA^{-1/2}\ \text{(or }+a_pA^{-3/4}\text{)}, & \text{even-even},\\
0, & A\text{ odd},\\
-a_pA^{-1/2}\ \text{(or }-a_pA^{-3/4}\text{)}, & \text{odd-odd}.
\end{cases}
\]

Dividing by \(A\),

\[
\frac{B}{A}
=a_v-a_sA^{-1/3}
-a_c\frac{Z(Z-1)}{A^{4/3}}
-a_a\frac{(A-2Z)^2}{A^2}
+\frac{\delta}{A}.
\]

The volume term gives approximately the same positive binding per nucleon for all sufficiently large nuclei because the nuclear force is short-ranged and saturates. For light nuclei, however, a large fraction of nucleons lie near the surface, so the surface term substantially reduces \(B/A\). As \(A\) increases, this surface penalty per nucleon decreases.

For very heavy nuclei such as \(^{238}\mathrm U\), the long-range proton-proton Coulomb repulsion becomes large. For nuclei with roughly fixed \(Z/A\), its contribution per nucleon grows approximately as \(A^{2/3}\). Heavy stable nuclei also require a neutron excess to reduce Coulomb repulsion, which increases the negative asymmetry-energy contribution.

Thus \(B/A\) first rises as the surface effect becomes less important, reaches a maximum near the iron region, and then falls as Coulomb repulsion and neutron-proton asymmetry dominate. The pairing term mainly causes small local even-odd variations and does not determine the overall trend.

\[
\boxed{
B=a_vA-a_sA^{2/3}
-a_c\frac{Z(Z-1)}{A^{1/3}}
-a_a\frac{(A-2Z)^2}{A}
+\delta;
\quad
\left(\frac BA\right)_{\!^{56}\mathrm{Fe}}
>
\left(\frac BA\right)_{\!^{238}\mathrm U}
\text{ because surface effects dominate at low }A,
\text{ while Coulomb and asymmetry effects dominate at high }A.
}
\]

