Assume observer 2 moves with velocity \(+u\hat{\mathbf z}\) relative to observer 1. Choose the trajectory to lie in the \(x\)-\(z\) plane. Observer 1 measures

\[
v_z=v\cos\varphi,\qquad v_x=v\sin\varphi.
\]

The Lorentz transformation is

\[
z'=\gamma_u(z-ut),\qquad x'=x,\qquad
t'=\gamma_u\left(t-\frac{uz}{c^2}\right),
\qquad
\gamma_u=\frac{1}{\sqrt{1-u^2/c^2}}.
\]

Consequently,

\[
dt'=\gamma_u\left(1-\frac{uv_z}{c^2}\right)dt,
\]

so the longitudinal and transverse velocity components measured by observer 2 are

\[
v'_z=\frac{v_z-u}{1-uv_z/c^2}
=\frac{v\cos\varphi-u}
       {1-\dfrac{uv\cos\varphi}{c^2}},
\]

\[
v'_x=\frac{v_x}
{\gamma_u\left(1-uv_z/c^2\right)}
=\frac{v\sin\varphi}
{\gamma_u\left(1-\dfrac{uv\cos\varphi}{c^2}\right)}.
\]

Thus the measured speed is

\[
v'=
\frac{
\sqrt{(v\cos\varphi-u)^2+
\dfrac{v^2\sin^2\varphi}{\gamma_u^2}}
}{
1-\dfrac{uv\cos\varphi}{c^2}
}.
\]

If \(\varphi'\) is the angle relative to observer 2's \(+z\)-axis, then

\[
\tan\varphi'
=\frac{v'_x}{v'_z}
=\frac{v\sin\varphi}
{\gamma_u(v\cos\varphi-u)}.
\]

The correct quadrant is obtained using

\[
\varphi'=\operatorname{atan2}
\left(v\sin\varphi,\,
\gamma_u(v\cos\varphi-u)\right).
\]

For the light-speed limit \(v\to c\), writing \(\beta_u=u/c\), the components give

\[
\cos\varphi'
=\frac{\cos\varphi-\beta_u}
{1-\beta_u\cos\varphi},
\qquad
\sin\varphi'
=\frac{\sin\varphi}
{\gamma_u(1-\beta_u\cos\varphi)}.
\]

These satisfy \(\sin^2\varphi'+\cos^2\varphi'=1\), and hence \(v'\to c\), as required by Lorentz invariance.

\[
\boxed{
\begin{gathered}
v'_{\parallel}
=\frac{v\cos\varphi-u}
{1-\dfrac{uv\cos\varphi}{c^2}},
\qquad
v'_{\perp}
=\frac{v\sin\varphi}
{\gamma_u\left(1-\dfrac{uv\cos\varphi}{c^2}\right)},\\[4pt]
v'=
