Let \(\beta=(k_BT)^{-1}\). Each adsorption site has three possible configurations:

\[
\begin{array}{c|c|c}
\text{configuration} & N & E\\ \hline
\text{empty} & 0 & 0\\
\text{strongly bound} & 1 & -\epsilon_0\\
\text{weakly bound} & 1 & -\epsilon_0/2
\end{array}
\]

Because the substrate exchanges hydrogen molecules with the gas reservoir, a site is described grand canonically. Its grand partition function is

\[
\Xi_{\mathrm{site}}
=\sum_s e^{-\beta(E_s-\mu N_s)}
=1+e^{\beta(\mu+\epsilon_0)}
  +e^{\beta(\mu+\epsilon_0/2)}.
\]

The probabilities of occupying the two bound states are therefore

\[
\theta_1
=\frac{e^{\beta(\mu+\epsilon_0)}}{\Xi_{\mathrm{site}}},
\qquad
\theta_2
=\frac{e^{\beta(\mu+\epsilon_0/2)}}{\Xi_{\mathrm{site}}}.
\]

Thus, the total coverage—the probability that a site is occupied—is

\[
\theta=\theta_1+\theta_2
=\frac{e^{\beta\mu}
\left(e^{\beta\epsilon_0}+e^{\beta\epsilon_0/2}\right)}
{1+e^{\beta\mu}
\left(e^{\beta\epsilon_0}+e^{\beta\epsilon_0/2}\right)}.
\]

For an ideal gas of hydrogen molecules,

\[
e^{\beta\mu}
=\frac{P\lambda_T^3}{k_BT\,q_{\mathrm{int}}(T)},
\qquad
\lambda_T=\frac{h}{\sqrt{2\pi m k_BT}},
\]

where \(m\) is the molecular mass and \(q_{\mathrm{int}}\) is the single-molecule internal partition function. Defining

\[
P_Q(T)=\frac{k_BT\,q_{\mathrm{int}}(T)}{\lambda_T^3},
\]

the pressure-dependent coverage is

\[
\boxed{
\theta(T,P)=
\frac{\displaystyle \frac{P}{P_Q(T)}
\left[
e^{\epsilon_0/(k_BT)}
+e^{\epsilon_0/(2k_BT)}
\right]}
{\displaystyle 1+\frac{P}{P_Q(T)}
\left[
e^{\epsilon_0/(k_BT)}
+e^{\epsilon_0/(2k_BT)}
\right]}
}
\]

assuming both adsorbed states are nondegenerate. Degeneracies \(g_1,g_2\), if present, multiply the corresponding exponentials.
